            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Rachel Carson</strong><br>\nRachel Carson was born on May 27, 1907, in Springdale, Pennsylvania. She grew up on a farm where she developed a deep interest in the natural world. Her mother was a schoolteacher and nurtured her curiosity about wildlife and the environment. Carson excelled in school and went on to study at the Pennsylvania College for Women, which is now called Chatham University, where she initially studied English but switched to biology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>College and Early Career</strong><br>\nAfter graduating college, Rachel Carson pursued her master's degree in zoology at Johns Hopkins University. Due to financial difficulties, she was unable to complete her doctorate and began working for the U.S. Bureau of Fisheries. Carson's ability to write about science in a clear and engaging way led to a full-time writing position. She wrote radio scripts and articles about marine life and the importance of sea conservation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Writing Success</strong><br>\nRachel Carson published her first book, \"Under the Sea-Wind,\" in 1941, which received praise for its descriptive writing style and accuracy. However, the book was not a commercial success until after her later works gained popularity. Her subsequent books, \"The Sea Around Us\" and \"The Edge of the Sea,\" established her as a gifted writer and a knowledgeable marine biologist.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
