            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Environmental Advocacy</strong><br>\nCarson is best known for her environmental advocacy. She was deeply concerned about the effects of synthetic pesticides on the ecosystem. Her research led her to question the widespread use of DDT and other chemicals. She believed that chemical companies were spreading disinformation and that the public needed to be informed about the potential dangers of these substances.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Silent Spring</strong><br>\nIn 1962, Rachel Carson published \"Silent Spring,\" a book that challenged the practices of agricultural scientists and the government. The book's title reflects the devastating effect of pesticides on birds and the resulting silence in regions where they once thrived. \"Silent Spring\" is credited with launching the modern environmental movement and eventually led to the nationwide ban on DDT for agricultural use.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Science and Policy</strong><br>\nRachel Carson's work had a significant impact on science and environmental policies. She testified before Congress in 1963 about the dangers of pesticides, which contributed to the change in public perceptions and policy. Her advocacy is partly responsible for the creation of the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) in 1970.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
