          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Challenges and Criticism</strong><br>\nDespite her accomplishments, Carson faced significant opposition from the chemical industry and some in the scientific community who criticized her for being alarmist. However, she defended her work with evidence and emphasized the importance of ecological balance and the precautionary principle in dealing with new chemical agents.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Honors</strong><br>\nRachel Carson's legacy is celebrated for her contributions to environmentalism. Numerous awards, scholarships, and honors bear her name, recognizing her role in increasing ecological awareness. Her home in Colesville, Maryland, has been designated a National Historic Landmark.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nRachel Carson died of breast cancer on April 14, 1964, at the age of 56. Despite her relatively short life, her influence endures. She was posthumously awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom by Jimmy Carter, and \"Silent Spring\" remains an influential work in environmental studies. Her life and work continue to be a touchstone for environmentalists around the world.</p>\n"
              },
